.he "fp (5)                       4/6/83                       fp (5)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
fp
- format used by the floppy backup service
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNOPSIS
.in +5
.br
cat fp.h
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
fp expects to interact with a block special device.
A block is 512 bytes long.
This format description is probably for the benefit
of system programmers and is technical in nature.
As you might notice, some ideas for this volume format
have been borrowed in modified form from Bell's "tp"
and the basic idea of a scalable unix file system.
We are grateful.

The following is the layout of each "fp" volume.
.sp
Block 0 is unused.
.br
Block 1 is the super block.
.br
Blocks 2 through (dsize - 1) are directory blocks.
.br
Blocks (dsize - 2) through vsize are data blocks.
.sp
The structure of the super block is as follows:
.in +5
.nf
.bd 7
struct super
	{
	unsigned short
		dsize,
		vsize,
		reel,		/* the reel number */
		flags,
		free,		/* next available free block */
		serial,
		check;

	unsigned long time;
	}
.in -5
.fi
dsize is the number of blocks in the volume directory.
.sp
vsize is the total number of blocks in the volume.
vsize is 800 for a Morrow double 5 in. floppy.
vsize is 2400 for a 1K double 8 in. floppy.
.sp
reel is the volume number.
Volume numbers start at 0.
.sp
flags is a word of boolean flags (see below).
.sp
free is the block number of the first available data block.
.sp
serial is the volume serial number.
It is used to ensure that the volume actually was
changed when that is the intention.
.sp
check is a checksum word such that the wordwise sum
of the super structure is zero.
.sp
time is the last modification time of the volume in seconds from the
epoch.
.sp
flags currently has the following defined bits.
.in +5
.ti -10
bit 0	CONTINUED
If this bit it set this is not the last volume of the set.
.in -5
.sp
As seen above, the data section of the volume
starts immediately after the directory section.
.sp
Each directory block holds 8 entries.
Each entry is 64 bytes long.
The following is the structure of a directory entry.
.in +5
.nf
.bd 7
struct entry
	{
	char name [PATHSIZE];
	short mode;
	short user;
	short group;
	long length;
	unsigned long time;
	unsigned char links;
	unsigned device;
	unsigned short block;		/* address on tape */
	unsigned short offset;		/* this chapter's block offset */
	unsigned char chapter;		/* chapter number */
	char flags;
	unsigned inumber;
	unsigned char major, minor;
	char instance;
	short checksum;
	}
.in -5
.fi
name is the file name, null terminated.
.sp
mode is the file mode, see flags in stat(2).
.sp
user is the user id number of the file.
It is a short so that version 7 group numbers will fit.
.sp
group is the group id number of the file.
It is a short so version 7 group numbers will fit.
.sp
length is the length in bytes of this part of the file.
.sp
time is the last modification time of the file
in seconds from the epoch.
.sp
links is the number of links to the file.
.sp
device is the major/minor device number if this entry represents
a special file.
If the entry does not represent a special file, then
the contents of "device" have no meaning.
.sp
block is the number of the first data block on the volume for this file.
If zero, then there is no data represented by this entry.
.sp
offset is the offset in blocks from the beginning of the original file
where this section of data resides.
.sp
chapter tells which part of the file this is.
chapter 0 is the first chapter of a file.
.sp
flags is a characters worth of boolean flags.
.sp
inumber is the inode number of the file.
.sp
major is the major device number of the device on which the original file
resides.
.sp
minor is the minor device number of the device on which the original file
resides.
.sp
checksum has a value such that the wordwise sum of the entry is zero.
.sp
Flags are as follows:
.in +5
.ti -10
bit 0 	CONTINUED
This is not the last entry for this file.
.ti -10
bit 1	LINK
This entry represents only a link, not the original file.
.in -5
.sp
Note that complete gross structure of the volume
is determined by the dsize and vsize entries of the super structure.
In constructing new volumes, "fp" applies the formula:
.nf
.in +5
dsize = vsize / 80;
.in -5
.fi
This is rather arbitrary.
The number was chosen to make current floppy directories fit
entirely in primary memory to for performance reasons.
.sp
Also, the only way to discover how many volumes comprise set
or how many chapter (or parts) comprise a file is by
encountering the last volume or the last chapter.
.sp
If the LINK bit is set in the flags member of an entry and
the associated file is a plain file, then the entry
has no associated data.  The only information it conveys
is the name and mode of the file.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
